# Git Branch Sync Preflight (Pivotal)

**Incident source:** [Capture Tools vanished on branch switch](cc661455-dcdb-4c5d-9778-b77cb128f922) — feature lived on `main` after merge, but checkout of an older PR branch (`pr-119-qp-helpers`) removed it from disk because that branch forked **before** the merge.

A branch is a **snapshot**. Checking out an old branch rewrites the working tree to that snapshot. Features already on `main` are **not deleted from GitHub** — they are simply absent from the older snapshot.

This rule is the **mandatory examination unit** before any Git voice command (`git commit`, `git commit all`, `git commit push`, merge, pull, checkout, PR review, `git workflow`).

---

## Hard rule

**Never leave the user on a branch that is behind `main` when they need features already merged into `main`.** Sync first, then continue.

---

## Preflight (run every time — before commit / push / merge / checkout / PR test)

Say out loud:

> Phase: git-preflight. Branch: `<current>`. Behind main: `<yes/no N commits>`. Action: `<sync | stay | abort>`.

Run in parallel:

```powershell
git fetch origin
git branch --show-current
git status --short
git rev-list --left-right --count origin/main...HEAD
```

| Result | Meaning | Action |
|---|---|---|
| `N 0` (behind main) | Current branch missing commits that are on `main` | **Sync** before testing or committing unrelated work (see below) |
| `0 M` (ahead only) | Fine — has unique commits | Proceed |
| `N M` (diverged) | Both sides moved | Sync with merge (default) or rebase only if user asks |
| On `main`, clean | Stable checkout | Proceed; create task branches from updated `main` only |

Also spot-check **canonical feature markers** when the user reports something “disappeared”:

| Feature | Marker path (must exist after sync) |
|---|---|
| Capture Tools | `extension/content/widget/widget.capture-menu.js` |
| Capture slice | `extension/content/capture/` |

If marker missing on current branch but present on `origin/main` → branch is stale. Sync. Do not re-implement.

---

## Sync playbook (old / PR / review branches)

When current branch is behind `origin/main` **and** the user will test the extension or continue work on that branch:

```powershell
git fetch origin
git merge origin/main
# resolve conflicts if any, then:
git push
```

Only after sync: reload the unpacked extension and verify markers exist.

**Do not** “fix” a missing feature by copying files from `main` without merging — that creates duplicate history and future conflicts.

---

## Commit / push / merge orchestration

1. **Implement on `main`** until SUCCESS (see `git-commit-branch-alignment.mdc`).
2. **Commit on a task branch** created from **fresh** `main` (`git pull origin main` first).
3. **Land on `main` via PR merge** (or explicit user-approved merge) — that is what makes a feature permanent.
4. After merge: `git checkout main && git pull origin main`.
5. **Any older open branch** the user will test next → run Sync playbook so it inherits `main`.
6. After `git commit all`: return to `main` (already required) **and** report if any open local task branches are still behind `origin/main`.

---

## Forbidden (causes the “disappeared again” loop)

- Checking out an old PR/review branch for testing **without** merging `origin/main` first.
- Assuming “merged to main” means every local branch has the files.
- Re-implementing a feature that already exists on `origin/main` because the current checkout is stale.
- Leaving the user on a stale branch after a successful `main` merge without warning them.

---

## User education (one short line when this fires)

When preflight finds a stale branch, tell Ezequiel:

> This branch forked before `<feature>` landed on `main`. Merging `origin/main` into this branch brings it back; the code was never deleted.

